steps.body:
Access modifiers decide who can use a field, method, or constructor. Java has four levels:

| Modifier | Who can use it |
| --- | --- |
| `private` | only code inside the same class |
| none (package-private) | every class in the same package |
| `protected` | the package, plus subclasses (see the Inheritance lesson) |
| `public` | every class |

All the classes in one file share a package, so here only `private` changes what other classes can see. The standard pattern: make fields `private` and give the class `public` methods. A getter reads a field. A setter changes it and can refuse bad values, so outside code cannot put the object in a bad state. A constructor that takes a starting value needs the same check.

```java
class Account {
    private int balance;

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
System.out.println(new Account().getBalance()); // 0
```

Now `acct.balance` in `main` is a compile error, while `acct.getBalance()` works. Getters are named `getX` (or `isX` for a boolean) and setters `setX`. Only add a setter when outside code really should change the field. A method with a clear purpose, like `deposit`, often beats a raw setter.

