steps.body:
A normal field is an instance field: every object gets its own copy. A `static` field belongs to the class, so there is exactly one copy, shared by every object. When one object changes it, all of them see the new value.

```java
class Ticket {
    private static int count = 0;  // one counter for the whole class
    private int id;                // one id per ticket

    public static int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}
System.out.println(Ticket.getCount()); // 0
```

A `static` method is called on the class, as in `Ticket.getCount()`, with no object needed. That is why `main` is `static`: Java calls it before any object exists.

The flip side is that a static method has no `this`. It cannot read instance fields like `id` or call instance methods on its own, because there is no object to read them from. Instance members always need an object, as in `ticket.getId()`. Writing `return id` inside a static method fails to compile with "non-static variable id cannot be referenced from a static context". Instance methods, on the other hand, can use static members freely. Java also lets you call a static method through an object, as in `a.getCount()`, but that hides the fact that it is shared, so always use the class name.

Use `static` for facts about the whole class, like a count or a running total, and instance fields for facts about one object.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Tickets: " + Ticket.getCount());
        Ticket a = new Ticket("Ana");
        Ticket b = new Ticket("Ben");
        Ticket c = new Ticket("Cy");
        System.out.println(a);
        System.out.println(b);
        System.out.println(c);
        System.out.println("Tickets: " + Ticket.getCount());
    }
}

class Ticket {
    private static int count = 0;

    private int id;
    private String owner;

    public Ticket(String owner) {
        count++;
        this.id = count;
        this.owner = owner;
    }

    public static int getCount() {
        return count;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Ticket #" + id + " for " + owner;
    }
}

