steps.body:
A `final` field gets its value once, in the constructor, and never changes after that. When every field is `final` and no method changes state, the class is immutable. Methods that would change it return a new object instead, just like `String` methods do. A small immutable class with named fields is Java's clean replacement for a tuple: a field named `dollars` says more than `pair[0]`.

A class can have several constructors with different parameters. That is constructor overloading. Chain them with `this(...)` so the real work lives in one place. The `this(...)` call must be the first statement in the constructor.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println(new Money(4, 75));
        System.out.println(new Money(2));
    }
}

class Money {
    private final int dollars;
    private final int cents;

    {{code}}

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return String.format("$%d.%02d", dollars, cents);
    }
}
```

```java
public Money(int dollars, int cents) {
    this.dollars = dollars;
    this.cents = cents;
}

public Money(int dollars) {
    this(dollars, 0);
}
```

A `static final` field is a class constant: one shared value that never changes, named in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE, like `Integer.MAX_VALUE`. It can even hold an object of the class itself.

Gotchas: each `final` field must be assigned exactly once by the time construction ends, directly or through `this(...)`, or the code does not compile. And `final` only locks the variable, not the object it points to, so items can still be added to a `final` list.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Money price = new Money(4, 75);
        Money tip = new Money(2);
        Money total = Money.ZERO.plus(price).plus(tip).plus(new Money(0, 50));
        System.out.println("Price: " + price);
        System.out.println("Tip: " + tip);
        System.out.println("Total: " + total);
        System.out.println("Price is still " + price);
    }
}

class Money {
    private static final int CENTS_PER_DOLLAR = 100;
    public static final Money ZERO = new Money(0);

    private final int dollars;
    private final int cents;

    public Money(int dollars, int cents) {
        this.dollars = dollars;
        this.cents = cents;
    }

    public Money(int dollars) {
        this(dollars, 0);
    }

    public Money plus(Money other) {
        int totalCents = (dollars + other.dollars) * CENTS_PER_DOLLAR + cents + other.cents;
        return new Money(totalCents / CENTS_PER_DOLLAR, totalCents % CENTS_PER_DOLLAR);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return String.format("$%d.%02d", dollars, cents);
    }
}

