steps.body:
An enum is a type with a fixed set of named values. Declare it after `Main`, like a class:

```java
enum Size { SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE }
System.out.println(Size.MEDIUM); // MEDIUM
```

Each constant is a `static final` object, so there is only ever one `Size.SMALL` and comparing with `==` is safe. The methods you will use most:

- `Size.values()` returns an array of every constant in declaration order.
- `s.name()` gives the text `"SMALL"`. Printing `s` shows the same.
- `s.ordinal()` gives the position, starting at 0. Do not store meaning in it, since reordering the constants changes it.
- `Size.valueOf("LARGE")` turns text into the constant. The name must match exactly, so `valueOf("large")` throws `IllegalArgumentException`.

In a `switch`, write the bare constant: `case SMALL`. Writing `case Size.SMALL` is a compile error in Java 15. A switch expression that lists every constant is exhaustive, so it needs no `default`, and if a constant is added later the compiler flags every switch expression that misses it.

Enums can carry data. Give them a `final` field and a constructor, and pass values in parentheses after each constant. The constant list then ends with a semicolon. An enum constructor is always private, even without the word, so only the constants can call it.

```java
enum Size {
    SMALL(3), MEDIUM(4), LARGE(5);

    private final int price;

    Size(int price) {
        this.price = price;
    }

    public int getPrice() {
        return price;
    }
}
System.out.println(Size.LARGE.getPrice()); // 5
```

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (Size s : Size.values()) {
            System.out.println(s.ordinal() + " " + s.name() + " $" + s.getPrice() + " (" + label(s) + ")");
        }
        Size picked = Size.valueOf("MEDIUM");
        System.out.println("Picked: " + picked);
        System.out.println("Is medium: " + (picked == Size.MEDIUM));
    }

    static String label(Size s) {
        return switch (s) {
            case SMALL -> "a little cup";
            case MEDIUM -> "the usual";
            case LARGE -> "a big one";
        };
    }
}

